# Kernel config addition needed for Bluetooth to work at all. This device's
# stock kernel .config has CONFIG_BT explicitly unset, so the running system
# has NO Linux Bluetooth stack whatsoever. See docs/bluetooth.md for the full
# evidence trail.
#
# THE SYMPTOM, found live and unambiguous:
#
#   $ hciconfig -a
#   Can't open HCI socket.: Address family not supported by protocol
#
# i.e. AF_BLUETOOTH does not exist. Downstream of that, and explained
# entirely by it:
#   - bluetooth.service   inactive
#   - bluebinder.service  failed, with "Error: code: 19 (No such device)"
#     ENODEV is exactly what opening /dev/vhci gives when CONFIG_BT_HCIVHCI
#     was never built. bluebinder had been sitting in `systemctl --failed`
#     unexplained since the LXC container first started running; this is why.
#
# WHAT WAS ALREADY IN PLACE (verified before changing anything, so that this
# change is the minimum necessary and not a guessed batch):
#   - CONFIG_SCSC_BT=y is ALREADY set in this tree. The Samsung SCSC combo-chip
#     Bluetooth driver is built in and working: /dev/scsc_h4_0 exists on the
#     running device (alongside /dev/scsc_ant_0 and /dev/scsc_log_collector_0).
#   - Samsung's own android.hardware.bluetooth@1.0-service is RUNNING inside
#     the LXC container (PID 122, init.svc.vendor.bluetooth-1-0 = running).
#   - So the entire Android/vendor half of the stack was already alive. The
#     ONLY missing piece was the Linux half.
#
# THE ARCHITECTURE THIS SUPPORTS (and why CONFIG_BT_HCIVHCI is the key option):
#   radio  ->  scsc_bt driver (/dev/scsc_h4_0)
#          ->  android.hardware.bluetooth@1.0-service   [in the LXC container]
#          ->  binder
#          ->  bluebinder                               [Droidian side]
#          ->  /dev/vhci                                [CONFIG_BT_HCIVHCI]
#          ->  Linux Bluetooth core / BlueZ             [CONFIG_BT]
#
# This keeps the radio driven by the vendor HAL, matching this project's
# reuse-the-vendor-implementation rule in CLAUDE.md.
CONFIG_BT=y
CONFIG_BT_BREDR=y
CONFIG_BT_LE=y
CONFIG_BT_HCIVHCI=y
CONFIG_BT_RFCOMM=y
CONFIG_BT_RFCOMM_TTY=y
CONFIG_BT_BNEP=y
CONFIG_BT_BNEP_MC_FILTER=y
CONFIG_BT_BNEP_PROTO_FILTER=y
CONFIG_BT_HIDP=y
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT SET: CONFIG_SCSC_BT_BLUEZ
#
# Samsung's scsc_bt Kconfig offers a "SCSC MX BlueZ support" option that makes
# the driver register its own hci device directly with the Linux stack. That
# would race Samsung's own Android HAL for the same chip - two owners, one
# radio - and bypass the vendor HAL path this port is built around. It is
# unset in the stock config and is left unset. Do not enable it "as well as"
# the above without a specific reason and a real test; it is not a belt-and-
# braces addition, it is a competing design.
#
# ALSO NOT NEEDED: any of the CONFIG_BT_HCIBTUSB / HCIUART / HCIBCM203X etc.
# transport drivers. Those are for real host-attached controllers. The only
# transport this device needs from Linux's point of view is the virtual one.
#
# CRYPTO DEPENDENCIES: CONFIG_BT selects CRYPTO, CRYPTO_BLKCIPHER, CRYPTO_AES,
# CRYPTO_CMAC, CRYPTO_ECB, CRYPTO_SHA256 and CRYPTO_ECDH itself via Kconfig
# `select` statements, so they are not listed here. VERIFY they actually got
# pulled in when checking the resolved .config - per this project's repeated
# lesson, Kconfig silently drops symbols whose dependencies aren't met, with
# no build error.
#
# VERIFY AFTER THE REBUILD (do not assume - check /root/kernel-mint/.config in
# the halium-build container, NOT /proc/config.gz or lxc-checkconfig on the
# device, both of which read a stale frozen IKCONFIG blob - see CLAUDE.md):
#   CONFIG_BT=y
#   CONFIG_BT_HCIVHCI=y
#   CONFIG_BT_RFCOMM=y
#   CONFIG_BT_HIDP=y
#   CONFIG_SCSC_BT=y                     (should be unchanged)
#   # CONFIG_SCSC_BT_BLUEZ is not set    (should be unchanged)
#
# VERIFY LIVE AFTER FLASHING:
#   - /dev/vhci exists
#   - hciconfig no longer errors
#   - bluebinder.service starts and stays running
#   - an hci0 device appears (hciconfig -a / bluetoothctl list)
#   - NO regression: Android LXC container still State: RUNNING, phosh still
#     active with 0 render errors, Wi-Fi still associates. The Wi-Fi and
#     Bluetooth halves share the same SCSC combo chip and the same wlbtd
#     daemon, so Wi-Fi is the specific thing most worth re-checking here.
#
# Apply the same way as kernel/07-kernel-config-lxc-networking.config and
# kernel/08-kernel-config-vt-display.config - as echo lines in build.sh's
# config-injection block.
